    date: '2026-07-08',
    readTime: 7,
    intro: 'Commercial pool cleaning accounts represent some of the most stable revenue in the pool service industry, but they come with higher compliance demands, more frequent visits, and a different client relationship than residential work. Understanding how to win commercial accounts, price them correctly, and deliver service that meets regulatory standards is the foundation of a successful commercial pool cleaning operation.',
    sections: [
      {
        h2: 'Types of Commercial Accounts and What They Require',
        content: 'Commercial pools span a wide range of settings, each with its own regulatory environment, usage patterns, and client expectations. Apartment complex pools typically operate from May through September in northern markets and year-round in warmer climates. They serve residents who may use the pool heavily during warm weather and sporadically otherwise. Property managers are the primary point of contact, and they\'re often measured by whether the pool generates resident complaints, which means water clarity and consistent availability are more important to them than understanding the chemistry details. HOA pools have a similar dynamic but with a board of directors rather than a single property manager as the decision-making authority. HOA contracts can be stable and long-term but may involve more stakeholders in the decision to hire and retain a service provider. Expect periodic board meetings, annual contract renewals, and occasional requests to present at a homeowner meeting. Hotel pools and resort pools have the highest regulatory standards of any commercial setting because they\'re accessible to the public, including children and guests with potential health vulnerabilities. Health department inspections are routine, and a failed inspection can result in a pool closure that embarrasses the property and generates significant pressure on the cleaning provider. Visit frequency requirements for hotels are often daily or even twice daily during peak season. Most commercial pools are subject to state or local health department regulations that specify minimum and maximum chemical levels, required testing frequency, record-keeping requirements, and inspection protocols. Before servicing any commercial account, obtain a copy of the applicable regulations and ensure your service protocol meets or exceeds every requirement. A CPO certification demonstrates that you\'ve been trained on these standards and gives commercial clients confidence in your qualifications.'
      },
      {
        h2: 'Visit Frequency and Compliance Requirements',
        content: 'Commercial pools almost universally require more frequent service than residential pools, and the frequency requirement is often defined by regulation rather than by operator preference. State health codes for public pools typically require testing at a defined minimum frequency, often daily, with records maintained on site for inspection. Some jurisdictions require twice-daily testing during peak operating hours, with each test result logged with the time, tester identity, and all measured parameters. Your service contract for commercial accounts should be built around compliance frequency rather than budget discussions. If the regulation requires daily testing, daily testing is what you provide, and your pricing reflects that scope. Offering a commercial client weekly testing to save them money isn\'t a value-add; it\'s a liability for both of you if the pool fails a health inspection. Beyond minimum regulatory requirements, commercial pools benefit from more frequent visits for operational reasons. A hotel pool with 50 to 100 swimmers per day consumes chlorine at a rate that can exceed what can be added during a single weekly visit. High bather load generates substantial organic demand, and maintaining adequate free chlorine requires either more frequent chemical addition or automatic dosing systems that supplement between service visits. Automatic chemical dosing systems are common in commercial pools and can dramatically reduce the manual chemical work required on each visit, allowing technicians to focus on physical cleaning and filter maintenance rather than chemistry adjustments. However, automatic systems require their own maintenance, calibration, and monitoring, which becomes part of your service scope. Including automation maintenance in your commercial service contract adds value and keeps the client from calling a separate technician for those tasks.'
      },
      {
        h2: 'Pricing Commercial Accounts and Navigating Contracts',
        content: 'Commercial pool cleaning pricing is typically calculated differently from residential pricing. Rather than pricing per visit or per month for a once-weekly service, commercial accounts are often priced as a monthly flat fee that covers all scheduled service visits, standard chemical additions, and routine maintenance tasks within a defined scope. This predictable monthly billing simplifies budgeting for the client and provides revenue stability for your business. Pricing a commercial account starts with calculating the total cost of service delivery: number of visits per month multiplied by your cost per visit including labor, drive time, and overhead, plus expected chemical cost, plus a margin that reflects the complexity and liability of commercial work. Commercial accounts should carry a higher margin than residential accounts to compensate for the greater compliance burden, the potential liability of a public pool failing a health inspection, and the additional administrative work of managing a commercial client relationship. Expect to price commercial accounts at 1.5 to 2 times the per-visit rate you\'d charge for a similar-sized residential pool, adjusted for visit frequency. Commercial contracts introduce legal elements that residential service agreements typically don\'t include: indemnification clauses, certificate of insurance requirements, minimum coverage amounts, and sometimes performance bonds. Have a lawyer review your standard commercial service agreement before executing your first commercial contract, and ensure your insurance coverage meets the minimum requirements you\'ll encounter in commercial client agreements. Renewal timing is also important: most commercial contracts run on annual or multi-year terms, and proactively discussing renewal 60 to 90 days before expiration gives you time to renegotiate pricing, adjust scope, and prevent losing the account to a competitor who gets there first.'
      }
    ],
